<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adnan Menderes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adnan Menderes was a Turkish politician who co-founded the Democrat Party and became prime minister after its victory in the 1950 election, the first peaceful transfer of power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Turkish Republic</a>. His government encouraged private enterprise and agriculture, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1952 and relaxed some secularist restrictions. Overthrown by the military coup of 1960, he was tried and hanged in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adnan-menderes.jpg" alt="Adnan Menderes" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adnan_Menderes_VI._Yasama_D%C3%B6nemi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali Adnan Menderes was born in 1899 in Koçarlı, Aydın, to a wealthy landowner of Crimean Tatar origin. He studied at the American College in İzmir and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>, fought the invading Greek army and received the Medal of Independence. In 1930 he organised a branch of the short-lived Liberal Republican Party in Aydın, and after it dissolved Atatürk himself invited him to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-peoples-party">Republican People's Party</a> (CHP); he was elected deputy for Aydın in 1931.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> he opposed statist policy. In June 1945 he joined Celâl Bayar, Fuat Köprülü and Refik Koraltan in the Motion with Four Signatures, which demanded more political freedom, and by September three of them had been stripped of CHP membership. The four founded the Democrat Party (DP) on 7 January 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The DP won 52 percent of the vote in the first free elections in Turkish history, on 14 May 1950, and Menderes became prime minister. He won again in 1954 and 1957. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> and was admitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1952. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a> aid, agriculture was mechanised and transport, energy and education advanced, though the economy slowed in the mid-1950s. He campaigned in 1950 on legalising the Arabic call to prayer, which had been banned.</p><p>His government instituted press censorship, had journalists arrested and tried to control the opposition parties and the universities. In September 1955, after a bomb exploded near the Turkish consulate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, mobs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> destroyed thousands of shops, houses, churches and graves of the Greek minority, killing over a dozen people. Several sources link the government to the Istanbul pogrom, and Menderes later apologised and offered compensation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A military coup on 27 May 1960 deposed the government. Menderes was tried on the island of Yassıada for violating the constitution, embezzlement and ordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> pogrom, and was sentenced to death. He attempted suicide with sleeping pills, and appeals from Cemal Gürsel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> and foreign leaders did not save him. He was hanged on İmralı on 17 September 1961, with the ministers Fatin Rüştü Zorlu and Hasan Polatkan. The Turkish Parliament pardoned all three on 17 September 1990, and his grave was moved to a mausoleum in Istanbul. Adnan Menderes University in Aydın and Adnan Menderes Airport in İzmir bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adnan Menderes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aydın, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in İmralı, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.85,27.85&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Aydın on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5245 803 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adnan Menderes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5695 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5695 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.85,27.85&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
